Mylar schrieb:
> 
> Back to the main topic, I want to clear up a few things that didn't
> work, in case someone digs this thread up in the future while
> searching for a solution to a similar problem.
> 
> Leprechauns will be referred to from this point on as lepers, as
> everytime I type Leprechaun I have to stare at it and spell it over in
> my head three or four times before I'm convinced that I have it
> correct.
> 
> The stage: friendly co-aligned priest inside his intact temple with
> ~60k of my gold, I was playing a chaotic male orc wizard.
> 
> Pet lepers and nymphs did not steal from the priest when confined in
> close quarters with him.

No wonder; they're much lower level. 

> Pet lepers and nymphs did not steal from the priest when confined in
> close quarters with him and under the effects of a ring of conflict.
> They attacked each other.

Bad tactics: confine _one_ leprechaun with the priest/ess, turn on
conflict, hope the leprechaun doesn't buy it. Using multiple helpers
when conflicting a single target just leads to infighting.

I tried it out in 3.4.1 wizard mode and conflict+pet+heavily stuffed
priest=quick refund. I'd be somewhat surprised if that behaviour had
been changed in the meantime (and i'm not in the mood to hunt my 3.4.3
executable down).

> I'm guessing the reason that this did not work is the level differance
> between the lepers and the priest.

Nope. The reason was that you confined several thieves together with
the priest, leading to counterproductive conflict targetting.

Conflict doesn't care about level differences.

> I'm not sure how
> strong a leper could get, or how you would get one to kill enough
> things to get there in the first place, so that might not even be
> possible.

AFAICT, leprechauns attack to steal gold, without doing any damage at
all. I.e., a pet leprechaun will not kill things, ever. The only way
to raise them in level seems to be potions of gain level (drop on
floor, wait for leprechaun to pick up and drink. Clonking them over
the head with it iirc only fully heals them without increasing their
level, and decreases their tameness). Of course, that method is
essentially unlimited - every item-using creature can get to level 49
this way.

> Things still left to try:
> 1. Raise a leper as a pet and see if I can get it high enough level to
> engage priests.

Good luck getting hold of the required potions.

> 2. Poly into a leper and try to steal the gold from the priest myself,
> I'm guessing this will piss off the priest and get me whacked in my
> puny, weak, leper form.

Yep. And you'll need some taming magic to approach the cleric again.

> Maybe use a helm to change alignment to
> something different from the priest first to avoid pissing off my god.

Bad idea. Putting on a helm of opposite alignment sets your protection
to zero, which defeats the purpose of buying _more_ protection.

